  "claims": [
    "A fixed chronological operation-label log marginal determines the executed word.",
    "Different neighboring operations can have equal final-state laws under checked read/write independence while logged laws differ in any fixed context.",
    "A Boolean chronological observer perfectly distinguishes any two different fixed operation words.",
    "An explicit involutive adjacent-label swap is a bijection transporting both full stores and operation-label histories between independent schedules.",
    "The inverse recovers the original recorded distribution; arbitrary correlated past/context is retained.",
    "Every target observation transports to a source observation; retaining the same observation requires an explicit invariance hypothesis."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Operation-label log only: no new implementation of full request/response transcript or native execution.",
    "Fixed operation words, not arbitrary adaptive schedulers.",
    "No claim that chronological histories or public clock observations are unchanged by actual execution reordering.",
    "No replacement of full-history security observations by an unannounced quotient.",
    "Read/write independence and probabilistic state kernels reuse existing ordinary exchange theorem.",
    "No full mixed tensor/copy/discard execution semantics or finite complete higher basis yet.",
    "No certificate-size or runtime advantage over ordinary shared proofs."
  ],
  "novelty": {
    "status": "unconfirmed",
    "interpretation": "An observation boundary and sufficient reversible correspondence, not claimed novel. Runtime-wire graphical state semantics is already covered by prior work; a contribution must survive comparison on cryptographic error, native contracts, local higher generation and repair."
  },
